{"tool_name":"abcd","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"abcd","home":"https://yunliweb.its.unc.edu/AbCD.html","license":"Not licensed","summary":"Providides pre-estimated effective sample sizes, specific to each minor allele frequency (MAF) category, for designs with arbitrary coverage (0.5 - 30X) and sample size (20-20,000), and for four major ethnic groups (Europeans, Africans, Asians and African Americans).","addition_date":"2017-08-20T14:56:04Z","last_update_date":"2020-01-19T17:47:47Z","tool_type":["Desktop application","Web application"],"collections":["Animal and Crop Genomics"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:abcd","credit":[{"email":"jiankang@umich.edu","name":"Jian Kang","orcidid":"http://orcid.org/0000-0002-5643-2668","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Contributor"]},{"email":"yunli@med.unc.edu","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"http://www.unc.edu/~yunmli/abcd.tutorial.html"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["animalandcropgenomics"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Data retrieval","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2422"}]}],"name":"AbCD","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"admin","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btt041","metadata":{"abstract":"Recent advances in sequencing technologies have revolutionized genetic studies. Although high-coverage sequencing can uncover most variants present in the sequenced sample, low-coverage sequencing is appealing for its cost effectiveness. Here, we present AbCD (arbitrary coverage design) to aid the design of sequencing-based studies. AbCD is a user-friendly interface providing pre-estimated effective sample sizes, specific to each minor allele frequency category, for designs with arbitrary coverage (0.5-30\u00d7) and sample size (20-10 000), and for four major ethnic groups (Europeans, Africans, Asians and African Americans). In addition, we also present two software tools: ShotGun and DesignPlanner, which were used to generate the estimates behind AbCD. ShotGun is a flexible short-read simulator for arbitrary user-specified read length and average depth, allowing cycle-specific sequencing error rates and realistic read depth distributions. DesignPlanner is a full pipeline that uses ShotGun to generate sequence data and performs initial SNP discovery, uses our previously presented linkage disequilibrium-aware method to call genotypes, and, finally, provides minor allele frequency-specific effective sample sizes. ShotGun plus DesignPlanner can accommodate effective sample size estimate for any combination of high-depth and low-depth data (for example, whole-genome low-depth plus exonic high-depth) or combination of sequence and genotype data [for example, whole-exome sequencing plus genotyping from existing Genomewide Association Study (GWAS)]. \u00a9 The Author 2013.","authors":[{"name":"Abecasis G.R."},{"name":"Huang K.-C."},{"name":"Kang J."},{"name":"Li Y."},{"name":"Wang Y."},{"name":"Xu Z."}],"citationCount":8,"date":"2013-03-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"AbCD: Arbitrary coverage design for sequencing-based genetic studies"},"pmcid":"PMC3597143","pmid":"23357921","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Population genetics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3056"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"AbCD","home":"https://bio.tools/abcd","license":"Not licensed","summary":"Providides pre-estimated effective sample sizes, specific to each minor allele frequency (MAF) category, for designs with arbitrary coverage (0.5 - 30X) and sample size (20-20,000), and for four major ethnic groups (Europeans, Africans, Asians and African Americans).","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:abcd/app/yunliweb.its.unc.edu","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"dis